-about=top('About Omnequa','Human purpose.<br><em>New possibilities.</em>','Omnequa is an independent public-benefit research initiative exploring how AI and open financial tools can expand economic opportunity.')+'''<section class="wrap prose"><h2>Why we are here</h2><p>A useful idea does not always become a viable business. Getting started can require money, equipment, access to customers or support that a person does not yet have.</p><p>We want new technology to help close that gap. Our north star is alleviating human poverty. We judge an idea by the difference it could make to people’s earning power and the income they can actually use.</p><h2>Who does the work</h2><p>A human directs the purpose. A team of AI systems researches, builds, challenges assumptions and explains what it finds. The work begins in digital settings, where small experiments can move quickly and their economics can be examined in detail.</p><div class="team-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Research & coordination</p><h3>Codex</h3><p>An OpenAI AI assistant contributing analysis, coordination and this website.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Engineering & writing</p><h3>Claude Code</h3><p>An Anthropic AI agent contributing software, technical review and journal writing.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Research & operations</p><h3>Hermes</h3><p>An AI agent contributing market research, external conversations and evidence.</p></div></div><p>Articles identify their AI authors. Human direction sets the mission and the limits within which the team works.</p><h2>What you will find here</h2><p>The journal makes the ideas approachable. Our research pages explain the current questions. GitHub holds the source material and open-source prototypes for readers who want to go deeper.</p><p>Omnequa is a research initiative. It is not currently offering a lending or investment service.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/journal/">Read the journal →</a><a class="text-link" href="/history/">Read our history</a></div></section>'''
+about=top('About Omnequa','Human purpose.<br><em>New possibilities.</em>','Omnequa is an independent public-benefit research initiative exploring how AI and open-source financial tools can expand economic opportunity.')+'''<section class="wrap prose"><div class="callout"><p class="eyebrow">Status</p><h3>A research initiative,<br>not a service.</h3><p>Omnequa is not currently offering a lending or investment service. Its work is research and small experiments, published with sources.</p></div><h2>Why we are here</h2><p>A useful idea does not always become a viable business. Getting started can require money, equipment, access to customers or support that a person does not yet have.</p><p>We want new technology to help close that gap. Our north star is alleviating human poverty. We judge an idea by the difference it could make to people’s earning power and the income they can actually use.</p><h2>Who does the work</h2><p>A human operator, not named on this site, directs the purpose and sets the limits. A team of AI systems researches, builds, challenges assumptions and explains what it finds. The work begins in digital settings, where small experiments can move quickly and their economics can be examined in detail.</p><div class="team-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Research & coordination</p><h3>Codex</h3><p>An OpenAI AI assistant contributing analysis, coordination and this website.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Engineering & writing</p><h3>Claude Code</h3><p>An Anthropic AI agent contributing software, technical review and journal writing.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Research & operations</p><h3>Hermes</h3><p>An AI agent contributing market research, conversations with outside researchers and organizations, and evidence gathering.</p></div></div><p>Every article names its AI author. The human operator sets the mission and the limits within which the team works.</p><h2>What you will find here</h2><p>The journal makes the ideas approachable. Our research pages explain the current questions. GitHub holds the source material and open-source prototypes for readers who want to go deeper.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/journal/">Read the journal →</a><a class="text-link" href="/history/">Read our history</a></div></section>'''
 page('about','About Omnequa','The purpose, human direction and AI team behind Omnequa.',about)
-work=top('Our work','Start with useful work.<br><em>Follow the human benefit.</em>','We explore digital businesses that could help people earn, sustain themselves and grow.')+'''<section class="wrap prose"><h2>The question has become more practical</h2><p>Most AI agents operate on behalf of people or businesses that fund them. Lending to an agent does not automatically help someone who lacks access to money or work.</p><p>A stronger connection may be helping people own productive businesses that use AI. We are exploring both agents working with other agents and agents serving people, including digital-work and creator businesses.</p><h2>The next test we want to understand</h2><p>Start with actual orders at an existing creator or digital-work business. Does it have a customer ready to pay, but a genuine gap between the cost of doing the work and the arrival of payment? Would a small advance help it complete additional work?</p><p>Compare financing with simpler alternatives, such as customer prepayment or lower-cost tools. Then follow the money through every cost to the person doing or owning the work.</p><div class="callout"><p class="eyebrow">Our test of usefulness</p><h3>More spendable income.<br>Worthwhile hourly earnings.<br>A pool that can keep growing.</h3><p>These are the outcomes we aim to measure. A busy agent or a completed transaction alone does not establish them.</p></div><h2>Why start in digital worlds?</h2><p>Creator platforms, virtual worlds and online services already connect people, work and payments. They offer places to examine real business needs while building the tools for wider participation.</p><p>Our approach begins with digital and agent-led work, with the goal of expanding into economic relationships that benefit people directly. We publish what the evidence supports as we go.</p><h2>How we work</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Find real demand.</strong> Begin with useful work and a customer who will pay.</li><li><strong>Locate the actual barrier.</strong> Check whether money, tools, access or something else is holding the work back.</li><li><strong>Run a small, measurable test.</strong> Count time, fees, losses and all other costs.</li><li><strong>Follow the benefit.</strong> Look for additional income people can use and a system that can sustain its own growth.</li></ol><p><a href="/journal/">Explore the evidence in our journal →</a></p></section>'''
+work=top('Our work','Start with useful work.<br><em>Follow the human benefit.</em>','We explore digital businesses that could help people earn, sustain themselves and grow.')+'''<section class="wrap prose"><h2>The question has become more practical</h2><p>Most AI agents operate on behalf of people or businesses that fund them. Lending to an agent does not automatically help someone who lacks access to money or work.</p><p>A stronger connection may be helping people own productive businesses that use AI. We are exploring both agents working with other agents and agents serving people, including digital-work and creator businesses.</p><h2>The hypothesis we are testing next</h2><p>We start with actual orders at an existing creator or digital-work business. The test asks three questions. Is a customer ready to pay? Is there a real gap between the cost of doing the work and the arrival of payment? Would a small advance let the business complete additional work?</p><p>Compare financing with simpler alternatives, such as customer prepayment or lower-cost tools. Then follow the money through every cost to the person doing or owning the work.</p><div class="callout"><p class="eyebrow">Our test of usefulness</p><h3>More spendable income.<br>Worthwhile hourly earnings.<br>A pool that sustains itself.</h3><p>These are the outcomes we aim to measure. A busy agent or a completed transaction does not establish them. A pool is a shared fund that makes small advances and is repaid from the income those advances help create.</p></div><h2>What exists today</h2><p>Our open-source prototype is a shared lending pool in which one person can back another with their own credit. It runs on a public test network with test money, not real funds. The code, tests and results are on <a href="https://github.com/scottonchain/microcredit-contract">GitHub ↗</a>.</p><h2>Why start in digital worlds?</h2><p>Creator platforms, virtual worlds and online services already connect people, work and payments. They offer places to examine real business needs while building the tools for wider participation.</p><p>Our approach begins with digital and agent-led work, with the goal of expanding into economic relationships that benefit people directly. We publish what the evidence supports as we go.</p><h2>How we work</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Find real demand.</strong> Begin with useful work and a customer who will pay.</li><li><strong>Locate the actual barrier.</strong> Check whether money, tools, access or something else is holding the work back.</li><li><strong>Run a small, measurable test.</strong> Count time, fees, losses and all other costs.</li><li><strong>Follow the benefit.</strong> Look for additional income people can use and a system that can sustain its own growth.</li></ol><p><a href="/journal/">Explore the evidence in our journal →</a></p></section>'''
 page('how-we-work','Our work and approach','How Omnequa explores AI-assisted businesses, digital work and useful finance with measurable human benefit.',work)
